WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:16.980
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Firstly, apologies for absence.
Patrick Carter is in hospital so can't be

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:24.140
with us today. We have Tim Ferris. He's here
but not here, if you know what I mean? But

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.070
he's on the telephone. Any declarations of
interest that haven't already been declared?

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:32.439
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: I have one David, which I
should just formally declare. My interest

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:38.020
now as the Interim Chair of Genomics England.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, thank you. Is everyone

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:43.390
happy with the minutes of the last meeting?
Yes. I don't think there's any matters arising

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:48.339
from the meeting, and so we'll move on to
the Chair's report. Dido, you want to say

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:51.309
a few words.
&gt;&gt; Dido Harding: Well, only very briefly.

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:56.930
As I know you do as well, we've both been
out and about across the system, across the

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:06.020
country as usual. I've been in Worthing, up
in Coventry, in Wigan, Luton, Chesterfield

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.350
yesterday with a number of us and a number
of other non–execs have been out together

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:16.320
in Bristol and I think a flavour of all of
our visits has been to focus on system working

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:23.229
in various different ways. Looking at both
the outstanding work people are doing on the

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:28.189
ground now, and I'd sort of, like to highlight
the work I saw actually with Bren in the audience

00:01:28.189 --> 00:01:36.090
in Wigan 10 days ago. Community work that's
really changing people's lives.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:42.430
Also, two events that I've attended in the
last six weeks since the last board meeting.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:50.000
One was the Sir Peter Carr Improvement Awards,
where we had eight different regional award

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.869
winners who had worked on system improvement
projects which were completely inspiring.

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:00.119
And I think really the challenge is encumbered
on us to work out how we help them spread

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:07.859
the work they're doing. Everything from virtual
diabetes clinics in Birmingham through to

00:02:07.859 --> 00:02:12.840
some fantastic work I saw being done in the
Royal Free by a consultant specialising in

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:20.570
sleep apnoea, where she reduced the waiting
time from first consultant appointment through

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:28.080
to diagnosis from 140 days to about 35, through
making more of the patient pathway digital

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.610
and virtual. So, lots of that in the Sir Peter
Carr awards.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:38.180
And then also Prena[?] as RNI[?] I gave out
awards at the Health Heroes awards last week,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:46.370
which was completely wonderful, recognising
some of the unsung heroes who've worked literally

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:53.230
all their lives. We had one of the – I think
she was the silver award winner, only silver,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:03:00.610
who had worked 50 years as a healthcare assistant
in the NHS. So, I just wanted to put on record

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.069
how inspiring it is when you get out and about
and meet people in their place of work and

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.390
then at these events, and I know I speak for
everyone – a number of us who've done all

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.540
these visits, how much we value and recognise
and applaud the work that people do.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.049
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, thank you very much Dido
and I think I'll just add to that, just to

00:03:17.049 --> 00:03:23.480
give two reflections. In the space of two
days, I went to a meeting in Cambridge where

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:29.590
it was really celebrating the 100,000 genome
project, you know, which is, you know – the

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:36.310
first of its kind in the world. And that technology
all came from Cambridge. And just seeing the

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:42.239
power of genomics and how it cost over $1
billion to sequence the first human genome.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:48.550
Now it's down well below £1000 and heading
down much lower than that as we go forward.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:56.400
And the day after that going to an NHS hospital,
going through their A&amp;E Department and just

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.610
seeing, you know, the incredibly hard work
that goes on from a whole range of different

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:06.610
specialities and skills in an A&amp;E Department.
I think it's one of the extraordinary things

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:12.360
about the NHS, is that on the one hand it
is at the leading edge of new clinical, sort

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.610
of, technology if you like, and on the other
hand it's delivering, you know, day–to–day

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:21.350
care of millions of people and I think that's
one of the extraordinary, rather inspiring

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.540
aspect of the NHS.
Now, I just want to go onto a slightly more,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:31.660
sort of, I was going to say boring, but it's
not boring – routine subject and just say

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:37.520
that on the subject of pensions, that we had
a board meeting, a board call on this on October

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.330
30th I think, and we agreed that the executive
will go away and work with the Department

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.100
of Health to try and come up with a solution
for the pensions. So, I'm just going to ask

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.680
Julian just to update the board on where we
are with that.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:55.530
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, following the board call
we announced last week and communicated to

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:06.789
the NHS the scheme that basically will provide
a contractually binding guarantee to clinicians

00:05:06.789 --> 00:05:12.009
that they will be compensated for the effect
of any tax bill arising from the annual allowance

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:19.280
upon their pensions. We are in the process
of finalising the details and looking to write

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:26.310
out to, in particular, all Trusts tomorrow
with effectively the letter to give effect

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:34.600
to that commitment. That's where we are. I
think that's probably enough now.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.229
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay, well, thanks Julian.
I know we're going to go through this in detail

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:43.759
and through it privately afterwards, but I
think our standing always requires that we

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:49.900
ask the board to ratify this subject to the
executive finalising the details, so we're

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.540
happy to do that. Excellent. Well, if we move
on to Simon, you report, please.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:06:00.520
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Okay, well thank you, David,
and picking up from both what you and Dido

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:05.039
said, I too want to thank the various people
that I've had the chance to spend time with

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:10.460
over the last several weeks since our last
meeting. Sheffield Children's Hospital, Poole,

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:16.740
Bart's, North Allerton, John Radcliffe in
Oxford, Healthwatch the NHS Providers Conference,

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:22.581
The Sun awards for brilliant staff across
the health service, the Royal College of Psychiatrists,

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:28.400
the Mayor of London and the director-general
of the World Health Organisation on the climate

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.650
emergency and clean air. So, a whole range
of activities, and that's true for everybody

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:37.240
sat around this table. I think this is a highly–engaged
group of people who are hearing directly from

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:43.160
our staff across the health service and the
patients as well. And so, we're obviously

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:47.230
in the middle of an election campaign. That
constrains how we have to conduct this board

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:52.730
meeting but suffice it to say that I think
we are all encouraged that the questions of

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:59.090
staffing are font and centre of that debate.
We've had – and I'm just now, sort of, reflecting

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:05.780
on publicly reported information, we've seen
that UCAS have reported a 6% increase in the

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:10.160
number of applications to study medicine,
the highest number we've ever had in this

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:19.349
country. And UCAS have also just reported
a 6.3% increase in nursing acceptances for

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:24.620
England for September, which means we'll have
the highest number of nurse starts even exceeding

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:29.680
the numbers we had before the nurse bursaries
were removed. But the fact is we are going

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.129
to need many more nurses and all of the main
parties are making commitments in that respect.

00:07:35.129 --> 00:07:39.319
So, one of the first conversations that Ruth
and I are going to be having with whoever

00:07:39.319 --> 00:07:45.240
is the government on December 13th will be
to say let's make sure that whatever you're

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:51.430
planning for nursing support and other staff
is well–communicated ahead of the January

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:57.680
15th UCAS deadline for people making their
applications for university for the following

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.659
year.
So, I think we are, as usual during this period,

00:08:02.659 --> 00:08:09.699
in dialogue and preparation for whatever the
electorate decide should be the case after

00:08:09.699 --> 00:08:14.080
December 13th. There are some other issues.
In the meantime, I think it might be worth

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:20.060
just hearing briefly from Steve on Shrewsbury
and Telford, given the importance of that

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:22.389
matter, and then anything else that you want
to discuss.

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:27.211
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes, and thank you, Simon.
So, on Shrewsbury and Telford. So, the board

00:08:27.211 --> 00:08:32.950
will be aware that in 2017 the Secretary of
State asked NHS Improvement to commission

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:37.380
an independent review into the handling of
incidents relating to the care received by

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:43.350
23 families and maternity units at Shrewsbury
and Telford Hospital NHS Trust. And Donna

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:48.840
Ockenden, a midwife with experience of handling
large–scale reviews was appointed as the

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:53.460
chair of the independent review.
So, this work at NHS Improvement was originally

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:59.470
overseen by my predecessor Dr Kathy McLean,
the Executive Medical Director and transferred

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.600
to me in May of this year when I took on the
joint role of Medical Director of NHS England

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:12.130
and NHS Improvement. Since the original commission
and media attention, more families have come

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:18.800
forward, both to Donna as chair of the independent
review and to the Trust in Shrewsbury and

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:26.331
Telford and indeed as part of their insurance
processes, at the end of last year, NHS Improvement

00:09:26.331 --> 00:09:32.900
looked into potential cases of concern within
Trust records. And so that has resulted in

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:37.780
an expansion in both the scale and the scope
of the original review.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:44.391
So, since May, we have been working hard with
Donna and the team and the Trust to increase

00:09:44.391 --> 00:09:51.820
the resource available to the independent
review in order for them to take on the additional

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:59.290
cases of concern, which now have grown to
over 600. So, I'm pleased to say that we've

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:04.690
worked closely and successfully and also with
the Department of Health and Social Care on

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:12.160
this matter and we have today released the
terms of reference which expand the scope

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:18.900
of the review with the agreement of DHSC.
We're working closely both with the independent

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:24.920
review but also with the Trust to ensure that
clinical records are released to the independent

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:31.671
review and I have asked Donna to work directly
with the Trust on the process of that, so

00:10:31.671 --> 00:10:37.300
that that fits with her work schedule as part
of the review.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:45.020
I personally met with the two families who
led the original group of 23 at the end of

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:51.600
July because I really thought it was important
that I heard first–hand from them. And I

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:57.310
should say that I am extremely grateful that
they took the time to meet with me, and I

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:04.370
was incredibly impressed by their determination
and their bravery. So, my team here at NHS

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:08.680
Improvement are continuing to work with the
Trust, with the independent review in collaboration

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:14.440
with DHSC and the families to ensure that
the independent review has all it needs in

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:21.180
order to undertake the work of the expanded
review in anticipation of a final report,

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:25.290
which is the wish of the families and the
chair, towards the end of next year.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:29.420
David Prior: Thanks Stephen. I'm just looking
at David Behan because you and I were involved,

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:35.350
you know, in the – after mid Staffs and
then at Morecambe Bay. I mean, are there any,

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:39.590
sort of, general comments you'd like to make
about these kinds of, you know, tragedies

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:43.150
that happen?
David Behan: I think just to pick up on Steve's

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:48.690
point about just listening hard to people's
experiences and actually absorbing those experiences

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:56.660
as it goes forward – as we think about what
the next steps are. At Steve's point, I was

00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:03.230
struck by the families that you and I met,
both their bravery but also their determination.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:10.260
Their determination to find out what happened
to them and why it happened. And I think that

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:18.330
driver around honest, open candour and communication
is so essential and people's frustration if

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:24.590
that's not happening to them just becomes
deeper. And I think it's at that point that

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:34.200
people, I think become more resilient. You
know, I don't know what it feels like to be

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.170
in that position and I think I'm occasionally
courageous at times, but I think in the face

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:43.970
of that kind of opposition it would be easier
to back off and give up rather than actually

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:49.020
see this through. So, I think that premium
about listening, and I think there's an awful

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:53.480
lot that can be learned from that very, very
active listening. And it's not just about

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:59.080
being available and meeting but it's actually
being seen to act on what people say, and

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:03.800
just to make the connection David, for the
work that you and I did, I think we changed

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:08.050
the whole number of things in the way that
we were approaching our work in CQC as a consequence

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:12.160
of listening to those conversations.
So, I think it's our ability to listen and

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:18.870
then process what people are saying and be
seen to act on it as well. And being patient

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:23.630
as well. I'm conscious that these stories
go back an awful long time and giving people

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:31.980
the opportunity to share their stories, but
also people want to be able to tell their

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:36.560
story as well, and that requires patience.
You can't rush that. If people are going to

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:41.320
feel listened to, then they need to be given
time as well, and that doesn't always sit

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:47.260
easily in a media context where people want
immediate answers. So, they'd be some of my

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:48.990
reflections.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes, I think – I mean, I

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:53.830
remember when we went up to Morecambe Bay
to meet some of the mothers who had lost children

00:13:53.830 --> 00:14:01.330
at Morecombe Bay. I mean, if you start hiding
behind an enquiry or procedures, then you're

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:05.410
lost. You've just got to go there. So, you
said you met some people – you met some

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:12.340
of the parents involved and I think we've
just got to be as open as you can. And I mean,

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:18.060
you know, this is just too important not to
be. I think we should just behave like human

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:27.240
beings, not sort of, hide behind administrative
processes and things. Anyone else wanted to

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:31.850
comment on that or not?
Simon have you got anything else you wanted

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:35.410
to – so, any other more general comments
on Simon's report?

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.310
&gt;&gt;Speaker: David, if I may?
David Prior: Sorry.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:45.250
&gt;&gt;Speaker: So, Simon last time we had a small
group of people who came to us from Swindon

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:52.020
who were very concerned with the general practice
service and I spent some time with them, and

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:58.260
we committed to do a number of things, one
of which was to ensure that the practices

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:04.790
were put onto a safe and secure basis. And
I just wonder if you could, just, sort of,

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:08.100
help us understand where we are with that?
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Sure. So, I think the – well,

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:15.780
I know the region that worked with the Swindon,
Wiltshire and North Somerset CCG I think it

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:22.160
is, to look specifically at the changes that
needed to happen in the GP practices, five

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:26.450
of whom I think that had gone through this
process and it was creating difficulties with

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:31.260
patients being able to get through on the
phone and book appointments. And subsequent

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:38.320
to that the CCG has arranged for all of those
practices to move to a different system. I

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:44.290
think several of the practices in Swindon
have now joined forces with the Great Western

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:48.810
Hospital in Swindon which also runs the community
services. So, I think about 30,000 of the

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:54.520
patients on practice lists are now joined
up with the hospital and the other practices

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:59.410
have all got an alternative solution that
has been developed with the CCG's. So, I believe

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:03.750
that that situation has been comprehensively
dealt with.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:09.200
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Okay, and I think the sort of,
lessons learned, I think are something that

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:11.840
we will come back to and give some thought
to over time.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.890
&gt;&gt;Speaker: Yes, the specific in this case
as I understand it was that the individual

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:23.910
practices were able to get into an alliance
with this third–party entity. This was not

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:30.720
something that was generated by the CCG or
the local NHS. But obviously what's changed

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.750
now is that we have primary care networks
and we would expect them to be a central part

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:39.110
of the conversation. If an individual practice
needs support over and above what it can secure,

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:43.690
then the PCN should be the vehicle for getting
that, rather than them having to try and go

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:47.360
find somebody else completely outwit that
dialogue.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:54.850
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right. Any other comments?
No. Alright, we'll move onto the next item

00:16:54.850 --> 00:16:58.600
then, please. Amanda, the operational performance?
&gt;&gt; Amanda Pritchard: Right, thank you very

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:05.620
much. So, I think to kind of, continue the
theme of just how hard we see people working

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:12.100
across the whole of the NHS and of course
all the partners as well, so not just local

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:16.970
government but voluntary sector too, we see
that particularly I think at the moment around

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:21.750
urgent and emergency care. So, I wonder if
we might start there and I could ask Pauline

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:25.060
– invite Pauline to talk to this particular
item.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:30.160
&gt;&gt;Pauline: Okay, so you've got the board paper
and I know everybody will have read it, but

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:35.190
just to highlight a couple of issues, and
I know the board is very much aware that at

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:41.340
the moment our focus is on supporting staff
to prepare for the winter periods, and indeed

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:46.960
the references made earlier to the pensions
issue and the staffing issues are really important

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:53.480
and very helpful as far as that is concerned.
When we reflect on where we are with performance

00:17:53.480 --> 00:18:00.620
at the moment, for October, our performance
was 83.6%, but our actual growth and attendances

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:10.550
was 4.4% and our emergency admissions were
up by 3.1%. I think importantly to note that

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:15.640
year–to–date if we look at the number
of patients that have been seen within four

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:21.630
hours, it's 212,000 more than for the same
period last year. So again, I think that's

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:28.070
a reflection of the work that our staff are
doing day in day out as far as urgent and

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:34.620
emergency care is concerned.
Just a few minutes to highlight a couple of

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:40.631
issues around our transformation agenda, again
relating to urgent and emergency care. We've

00:18:40.631 --> 00:18:48.430
now got 125 urgent treatment centres that
have been designated. 102 of them are able

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:55.610
to take direct bookings from 111 and we will
have 50 more designated by the end of December.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:19:02.960
Again, I think it's worth noting that those
bookable appointments into the urgent treatment

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:08.940
centres, even though it may be in the exact
same building, the exact same desk as before,

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:12.970
are no longer counted within our four–hour
performance. So, it's work that we're doing

00:19:12.970 --> 00:19:18.630
that would previously have been counted but
it's absolutely the right offer for our patients

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:24.540
and I think it's a significant success story,
thanks again to the hard work of the people

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:30.910
that have put it in place.
With regards to zero length of stay, and for

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:35.260
zero length of stay we're really talking about
same–day emergency care, we've talked about

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:41.440
it at previous meetings, and talking about
being able to care for patients without them

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:47.340
needing to stay in hospital overnight. If
we look at our performance from April to September,

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:54.320
it grew by 7.4% against the previous year,
whereas our length of stay for patients over

00:19:54.320 --> 00:20:02.180
one night only grew by 1.5%. So, again, I
think that's evidence of a changing care model.

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:08.220
And 90% of providers are now on track for
being able to offer that service 12 hours

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:16.930
a day, 7 days a week by the end of 2019, which
is up from 75% in March.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:23.970
Continuing the theme of transformation, from
a 999 perspective, despite the fact that there

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:29.360
were 67,000 more calls during the month of
October, we've seen this ongoing improvement

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:36.660
in performance for category one and category
four patients. And for 111 we've seen an ongoing

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:43.200
increase in the number of patients who receive
clinical input when they contact 111 if we

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:54.190
compare October to October. Last October was
52.1%, this October is 53.7%, so again, real

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:57.500
progress.
And at this time of the year I think it's

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:03.770
also important to mention that as far as our
flu vaccination programme is concerned, that

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:09.320
the up–to–date information on that is
that we have vaccinated more people than at

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:16.430
the same time last year. Our numbers as we
speak are 9.38 million and same time last

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:21.190
year 9.12. So, again – and particularly
patients over the age of 65. So, that's what

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:26.850
I was going to say about urgent and emergency
care, but I'll move to elective care if that's

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:30.640
okay? Yes.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: [Inaudible]. Ruth maybe you

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:35.750
could just give us a feel for what it's like
out there for nurses at the moment.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:41.230
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: I think it's really tough out
there right now. We've got nurses willing

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:49.220
to work additional shifts in the main, and
they are. And I'm grateful that many of them

00:21:49.220 --> 00:21:56.060
are. We're seeing nurse leaders working extremely
hard to look after the well–being of staff,

00:21:56.060 --> 00:22:03.420
not just them turning up in caring for patients.
So, I'm just hearing lots of stories. I was

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:08.150
speaking to many directors of nursing this
week actually where they're working – they've

00:22:08.150 --> 00:22:12.850
got a lot of challenges. They've got some
challenges in particular areas, but at the

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:18.540
end of the day they're working as teams, but
they are very challenged right now.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:23.090
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Pauline?
&gt;&gt;Pauline: Thank you. So, just moving on to

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:29.840
elective care and OTT. Our performance for
the month of September was 84.8% against the

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:39.190
18–week target. The waiting list at that
point grew by 9,000 and that's excluding non–reporters.

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:44.990
But I think the important point to make again,
as far as elective work is concerned, is that

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:52.390
when we look at our year–to–date performance
our elective admissions are up 2.6%, so that

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:59.350
means about 111,000 more. So, again, the system
working harder and are seeing more patients

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:06.130
and caring for more patients; against our
52–week target we've seen a 63% reduction

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:13.630
against June 18. So, for both of those areas,
a focus by staff on delivering better and

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:18.670
better care.
With regard to elective care, we've got a

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:26.340
significant transformation programme delivering
a major focus now on outpatient redesign,

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:32.530
and ongoing rollout of our capacity alerts.
The development of a specification for advice

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:38.540
and guidance. This is for GPs being able to
get advice and guidance rather than having

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:44.510
to refer a patient being able to get advice
from secondary care providers, etc. And our

00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:51.660
first-mover sites for the 26–week choice
offer have now been launched. We've got seven

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:57.590
sites. So again, big focus on transformation
as well as day–to–day delivery. I think

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:01.860
that just about covers those sections of the
report, Amanda.

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.540
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, Steve, a view just on the
sort of, transformation aspect of what Pauline's

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.940
been talking about in terms of the medical
workforce.

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:11.800
&gt;&gt; Stephen Powis: Yes, so actually I wanted
to start with pensions which we've already

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:19.050
talked about because I know from talking to
many doctors who ordinarily at this time of

00:24:19.050 --> 00:24:24.651
year would what to put in additional hours
in order to deal with the pressures that we

00:24:24.651 --> 00:24:29.890
traditionally see building as we head into
winter, but have felt constrained to do so

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:36.650
because they are worried that they would face
a tax bill that was not proportionate to the

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:40.950
extra hours that they were putting in, and
because of the complexity that led to uncertainty

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:47.200
over those tax arrangements. And so, I'm delighted
that Julian and the team here have worked

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:57.550
on a simple solution that we have now made
public that will allow doctors to do those

00:24:57.550 --> 00:25:04.310
extra hours without having to worry about
the potential consequences. And doctors want

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:08.800
to do the extra work for a couple of reasons.
Primarily, of course, for patients because

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:13.260
first and foremost, all medical professionals
and healthcare professionals care about patients,

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:19.180
but also because as things get busier, they
want to support their colleagues and to ensure

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:23.660
that whether it's the doctor in the department,
it's the nurse, it's the healthcare assistant,

00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:27.930
it's a pharmacist, that they are working as
a team together supporting each other. And

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:35.210
so, I would urge my fellow doctors and others
to not feel constrained now with the worry

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:40.500
that they can't do those additional shifts.
Especially as we come into the peak winter

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:48.290
period in the urgent and emergency care pathways.
But also, in elective care, as Pauline has

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:55.750
said, where it's important that we can put
on where possible, extra lists in order to

00:25:55.750 --> 00:26:01.030
ensure that we get through procedures, operations
that we need to get through.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:05.920
I think in terms of the wider transformation
– Pauline mentioned outpatients. Well, the

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:10.690
Royal College of Physicians wrote a report
this time last year that I had the privilege

00:26:10.690 --> 00:26:17.960
of writing the foreword to on outpatients,
and my memory of reading that report was both

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:23.300
the healthcare professionals that were spoken
to and the patients who use outpatients both

00:26:23.300 --> 00:26:29.680
thought it was a – something that was broken.
It was a mode of delivery that needed to be

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:34.900
updated for the 21st–century and transformed.
So, I think when patients are telling you

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:41.260
that and staff are telling you that together,
then you really do have the message that the

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:45.000
opportunity is there to do something different.
And certainly, when I go around the country

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:52.160
talking about reducing outpatient appointments
to deliver care closer to home, to save patients

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:58.700
having to travel in for appointments where
we could do it more effectively, that is enthusiastically

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:05.060
heard and Dido, you gave an example in your
introduction of how that can be translated

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:10.180
in a single scheme into reality. So, I think
there's real enthusiasm. I don't underestimate

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:15.520
the task because these are big change programmes,
but I think it's absolutely right that we

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.450
do this.
It's not just digital transformation, although

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:24.030
that helps. It's actually doing things once,
doing it right. Whether it's a one-stop clinic,

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:28.280
whether it's a consultation by telephone or
text. The tools are there, and the willingness

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:34.620
is there to do it. And of course, as we always
say, because around, we estimate about 5%

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:42.830
of travel in England on any one day is NHS–related,
reducing the number of journeys into outpatients

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:47.309
is part of our contribution to the carbon-neutral
environment, which of course benefits our

00:27:47.309 --> 00:27:53.630
respiratory patients as well who are particularly
affected by poor air quality. So, I think

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:58.500
this and other transformation programmes,
this is absolutely the time to do it and I'm

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:00.309
confident that clinicians will get behind
it.

00:28:00.309 --> 00:28:06.059
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ara, this is pretty much in
line with what you are doing back in 20007/8

00:28:06.059 --> 00:28:09.400
really wasn't it? When you were a minister?
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: In terms of the –

00:28:09.400 --> 00:28:12.890
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, in terms of trying to
deliver care outside big hospitals.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:17.730
&gt;&gt;Ara Darzi: Yes, yes, yes, and I remember
at the time I miserably failed, so I'm very

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:25.150
optimistic 10 years later that we have the
right leadership, and the right sort of, skills.

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:30.630
Because all our patients – I don't think
I know anyone who wants to go to a hospital

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:35.720
if – the provision of care nearer to the
patient – with the exception of serious

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:43.190
emergencies obviously. But I think as Steve
said the technological platform is available

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:49.320
to us now. The skilled staff that we have
in a community setting. The investment that

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:56.460
NHS England is making in primary care physicians
and nurses, if you bring all of this together.

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:03.360
Considering that 10 years on we have more
patients with chronic disease, and the last

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:09.440
thing they need is hospital care – in coordinative
care in the community with hospital supervision.

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:16.240
So, I'm very excited and I have no doubt Steve
will have a much better success out of this

00:29:16.240 --> 00:29:17.240
than I ever did.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Amanda did you want to carry

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:20.570
on? Thanks Ara. Do you want to carry with
your report?

00:29:20.570 --> 00:29:25.690
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you. Yes. So, I
think again, the theme that colleagues have

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:32.080
just talked about, which is sort of, responding
to the increase in the need for our services

00:29:32.080 --> 00:29:38.500
now, but at the same time absolutely putting
the energy into redesigning, reforming, transforming

00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:42.550
services, so that ultimately what we are able
to do is provide things that are better for

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:47.380
our patients and better for our – for the
people who work in the NHS as well, is very

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:52.010
much what we're all committed to doing. And
I did just want to – I'm assuming everybody

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:56.740
is read the paper, but just highlight one
or two areas where I think we've got some

00:29:56.740 --> 00:30:01.910
really very positive signs.
So, in relation to cancer, I think we talked

00:30:01.910 --> 00:30:08.160
last time about the targeted lung health checks
which is, of course, one of the big transformation

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:13.570
opportunities around trying to identify those
people at risk of lung cancer diagnosed at

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:19.250
an earlier stage. But just being much more
creative about taking those, kind of, diagnostic

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:22.910
opportunities to the places where patients
will be rather than to pick up on exactly

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:27.270
the theme that we were just talking to, expect
people always to come into our hospitals for

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:30.970
these things. So, supermarket car parks being
a great example.

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:36.910
We've also got work underway, led again by
the cancer team to implement the rapid diagnostic

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:41.640
centres. So, one for every cancer alliance
is the commitment. We already have nine up

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:46.990
and running and a lot of work going on now,
now that we've agreed the specification to

00:30:46.990 --> 00:30:50.760
roll those out.
In terms of activity, I did say, of course

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:56.900
that's the kind of, future focus in the here
and now. Again, for cancer actually we have

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:06.309
record-breaking levels of urgent cancer referrals
but also cancer treatments again up. So, we

00:31:06.309 --> 00:31:12.580
have had about 12.2% more referrals in the
12 months of September this year compared

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:17.170
with last year, but against that our two-week
wait performance has increased against last

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:23.470
months and the number of patients treated
within 62 days has gone up by 3.6%. So, that's

00:31:23.470 --> 00:31:30.750
over 5,500 people being treated now within
62 days compared with last year. So, I think

00:31:30.750 --> 00:31:36.740
final point to say on cancer is – and all
of that whilst also having cancer patient

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:41.500
experience at record high levels. So, that's
a huge testament to all of the people who

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:47.630
are working within cancer services, that despite
that rising demand and need, we're maintaining

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:53.090
that focus on quality and on patient experience
whilst also beginning to transform the service

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:56.299
at the same time.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Why don't we just stay with

00:31:56.299 --> 00:32:00.600
cancer? Michelle from your perspective at
CRUK there's a lot going on in new treatments,

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:04.620
new diagnostics. Do you want to give a –
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: It just really important

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:09.570
to hear some of the different progress and
work that's happening that Amanda talked about.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:13.809
I think very, very encouraging signs. If you
go to Manchester and see some of the lung

00:32:13.809 --> 00:32:18.580
health checks and the community outreach that's
happening there it's very positive. Because

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:24.620
we know lung cancer remains – incidence
and prevalence far too high. The work that's

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:30.950
going on with the RDC's at the moment is very,
very positive and of course, more people are

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:37.730
being treated now than ever before. But we
do face some challenges still in terms of

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:48.910
the 62-day wait, in terms of how we compare
to other comparable international countries

00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:53.980
in a number of specific cancers. And of course,
we know – and what's probably a hugely exciting

00:32:53.980 --> 00:33:01.190
area of the long-term plan is we have ambitions
to significantly increase survival of cancer

00:33:01.190 --> 00:33:08.040
through a whole range of measures including
a stage shift in early diagnosis, improvements

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:14.730
to screening and of course through the research
development and technology which we expect

00:33:14.730 --> 00:33:22.040
will enable us all to detect and prevent cancer
earlier. So, really good news, but more to

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:24.910
do.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: How is your campaign going

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:30.920
on around obesity and cancer?
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Well, it was a very topical

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:39.230
campaign, because of course what we do know
is that obesity links to 13 types of cancer.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:45.620
It raised significant media profile. In fact,
we won an award yesterday for the bravest

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:52.390
campaign in marketing in the UK this year.
But we know we've got a long way to go. Because

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:57.770
really the purpose of that campaign was not
only to increase public awareness, which was

00:33:57.770 --> 00:34:04.170
very low about the links between cancer and
obesity but to also persuade policymakers,

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:09.110
the people who will hold the keys to number
10 and who are in power in Scotland, Wales

00:34:09.110 --> 00:34:16.250
and Northern Ireland, to have a big shift
in policy around obesity too. So, we've been

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:24.139
calling for changes to packaging, multi–price
buy, and packaging. And we continue to push

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:28.870
incredibly hard on smoking cessation, which
of course remains a stubborn issue and we

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:31.630
won't be happy until we see a smoke–free
UK.

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:35.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I think you would agree with
that Simon, yes?

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:41.490
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens. Yes, defined I think as 5%
is the ASH definition and we need to see a

00:34:41.490 --> 00:34:45.350
faster–rate progress on smoking cessation
and there are all kinds of ways in which we'll

00:34:45.350 --> 00:34:49.139
be wanting to support that.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: And on the obesity as well?

00:34:49.139 --> 00:34:55.590
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes. On obesity as well.
That is undoubtedly true, not just for cancer

00:34:55.590 --> 00:34:58.920
but for the type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular
disease. There are a set of things obviously

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:04.810
the NHS has been doing around the DPP rollout,
but other programmes that we are going to

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:08.850
be investing in as part of the long-term plan.
And it would be our expectation that we would

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:13.011
bring forward the long-term plan implementation
framework either at the back end of this year

00:35:13.011 --> 00:35:16.580
or the very beginning of next, and that would
include those kinds of interventions.

00:35:16.580 --> 00:35:19.450
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right.
&gt;&gt;Speaker: I would just add one further point,

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:24.720
and Simon and others have talked about it.
The significant and the very welcome debate

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:30.240
more broadly on investing in workforce which
is critical to the achievement of the long-term

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:34.020
plan in a number of areas, but particularly
so in cancer too.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:37.340
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Amanda?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Right. Thank you. And

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:41.960
actually, on that workforce theme, something
I just did want to pull out of the report

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:48.810
again was that – so I think there's an absolute
commitment and clarity that one area where

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:53.800
we absolutely need to see continued growth
in the workforce is general practice and also

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:59.050
primary care. So, what we have seen so far
is a small increase in the number of doctors

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:05.180
working in general practice and a larger increase
in the wider primary care workforce since

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:10.040
last year, but that continues to be an area
where we're giving an awful lot of energy

00:36:10.040 --> 00:36:14.820
and effort and attention, because I think
we again recognise that just as we see the

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:19.670
pressures that we've just described on secondary
care around urgent emergency care cancer,

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:25.450
actually that's equally true in general practice
in primary care and that investment in the

00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:30.140
workforce and support for now the good news
that's already been referenced that we have

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:37.690
99% of the country now covered by primary
care networks. So, we have more than 99% of

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:42.869
general practices coming together to form
more than 1200 primary care networks across

00:36:42.869 --> 00:36:47.990
the country. So, that is a great vehicle for
us now to be able to support primary care

00:36:47.990 --> 00:36:54.609
going forward and primary care very much then
at the heart of the systems as they work into

00:36:54.609 --> 00:36:59.920
the future, but absolute recognition that
we do need to continue to invest in workforce

00:36:59.920 --> 00:37:04.440
that we know we still need.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Sorry, just – Ian are you

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:08.420
happy with primary care networks? You were
one of the architects of primary care networks.

00:37:08.420 --> 00:37:14.470
Are they going as you wanted them to?
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Yes, I mean it's early days.

00:37:14.470 --> 00:37:20.830
They've only just formed formally from 1st
July and there's obviously a lot of work that

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:26.590
those networks have got to do to get their
relationships right between constituent practices

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:33.320
and critically with their wider NHS and community
partners and I think our big focus, exactly

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:38.690
as Amanda said, at the moment is on helping
them be able to make use of their funding

00:37:38.690 --> 00:37:45.780
entitlements this year and indeed in future
years for additional staff. So, we have promised

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:53.170
that every network can secure a social prescriber
this year and recruitment is kind of, ploughing

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:58.030
ahead on that and similarly, pharmacists.
So, that's our immediate focus this year.

00:37:58.030 --> 00:38:02.060
And we are working with the British Medical
Association and the Royal College of General

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:08.020
Practitioners on that.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Great. Just two final

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:13.060
areas if I may? I did want to just talk a
bit about mental health and also just touch

00:38:13.060 --> 00:38:17.150
on learning disabilities.
So, in relation to mental health, clearly,

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:22.280
there's been a very significant commitment
in additional funding for mental health. We

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:27.930
do know that all CCGs reported that they have
met the mental health investment standard,

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:33.510
and what that has enabled is a really, really
significant and important increase in the

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:37.980
number of people who are able to access mental
health services. So, a particular example

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:44.460
to highlight is the fact that last year, of
course, we saw 13,000 women through perinatal

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:50.330
mental health services, which was significantly
more than the 9,000 target that we'd started.

00:38:50.330 --> 00:38:55.730
But that is just one example of what is now
a huge amount of activity going on within

00:38:55.730 --> 00:39:00.170
mental health to increase access and to develop
new services. And one of the things that we're

00:39:00.170 --> 00:39:05.030
continuing to do, Julian leading in particular,
as we think about planning for next year,

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:10.120
is ensuring that that same increase in investment
around mental health is reflected in local

00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:12.516
plans for 2021 and beyond.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Yes.

00:39:12.516 --> 00:39:13.970
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Shall I go onto the learning
disabilities?

00:39:13.970 --> 00:39:21.280
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No, no. Any questions on mental
health? Am I right now Amanda that every A&amp;E

00:39:21.280 --> 00:39:27.070
Department in the country has a mental health
liaison team in it? Or is that not quite right

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:30.290
yet?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Yes, yes, they do. But

00:39:30.290 --> 00:39:35.670
there's still, I guess – so we have the
Core 24 standards which are being rolled out

00:39:35.670 --> 00:39:39.700
across the country but every – certainly
every emergency department has a mental health

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:43.119
liaison team as part of it. Do you want to
say any more about that Pauline?

00:39:43.119 --> 00:39:46.460
&gt;&gt;Pauline: Yes and no I don't[?] think that's
accurate Amanda [?]. I think probably the

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:51.590
extent of the coverage, we still have some
way to go. But yes, we have rolled it out.

00:39:51.590 --> 00:39:55.360
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes.
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: So, just finally in relation

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:59.190
to learning disabilities. Because again I
think quite rightly what we've done in the

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:04.410
paper I think is celebrate the work to date.
So, you know, it's a significant reduction

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:10.119
since March 2015 in the number of people being
looked after in an inpatient setting. So,

00:40:10.119 --> 00:40:16.119
22% reduction. But an absolute commitment
to go further. I think it's not just clearly

00:40:16.119 --> 00:40:23.070
about the inpatient end. It's also about making
sure that the care education treatment reviews

00:40:23.070 --> 00:40:28.730
are undertaken, because that's such a critical
part of effective planning around people's

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:34.510
care. So, again, the good news is that the
number of those continues to grow year–on–year,

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:40.580
but I think that we would all recognise that
learning disabilities is an area that absolutely

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:45.000
requires that continued focus. And this is
a whole system effort because this is not

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:50.250
this is not just about health. This is absolutely
about a very close partnership with local

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:55.950
government, with social care, voluntary sector
as well, to look after what is in practice

00:40:55.950 --> 00:41:01.960
some of our most vulnerable people.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Is there any reducible minimum

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:05.180
that we're assuming of the figure? That figure
won't come down to zero, I think, will it?

00:41:05.180 --> 00:41:10.619
Or do you – is there a figure.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: No, I don't think the figure

00:41:10.619 --> 00:41:15.720
will come down to zero in terms of the number
of people who do at some point require specialist

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:20.580
inpatient support for learning disability
and autism but obviously we are aiming at

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:26.540
35% as the first milestone and then 50% by
23, 24, and there are parts of the country

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:31.450
that are already doing that. And a proportion
– I think about a quarter of people who

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:37.869
are in inpatient settings at the moment are
on an MOJ restriction, but we believe that

00:41:37.869 --> 00:41:42.360
with community alternatives then community
forensic support can sometimes provide an

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:48.300
alternative to that. And I have commissioned
Anne Longfield the Children's Commissioner

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:56.599
to chair the task force on inpatient learning
disability and autism services. I met with

00:41:56.599 --> 00:42:00.160
her this week in fact and she's going to be
looking not only at the inpatient element

00:42:00.160 --> 00:42:05.070
of that, but the upstream and the discharge
elements too. But there's an awful lot more

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:10.840
to be done here. Some of this is about workforce
questions, some of this is about the join

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:17.040
up with special educational needs and children's
social care services and Anne Longfield as

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:23.470
a clear independent champion will help drive
that agenda with us and for us.

00:42:23.470 --> 00:42:29.350
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Andrew, any comments
you'd like to make from your perspective at

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:32.201
Frimley?
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: In terms of?

00:42:32.201 --> 00:42:34.050
&gt;&gt;David Prior: In terms of how the system
is working.

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:40.900
&gt;&gt;Andrew Morris: So, I think it's really tough
and people are working really hard. The big

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:48.320
win that we've got now is the pensions breakthrough
and I think we will get a lot of clinical

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:56.760
buying in terms of covering more shifts at
consultant level, which will hopefully cope

00:42:56.760 --> 00:43:03.290
with an increase in activity and lift the
performance and, you know, we're all chasing

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:10.970
for hours. Everyone wants to clear an emergency
department. If you get a full emergency department

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:16.310
that's got, you know, 120 people and you're
normally handling around 60, you're under

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:21.530
a lot of pressure, and everyone's trying to
focus on getting those people treated and

00:43:21.530 --> 00:43:29.050
sorted and admitted appropriately or discharged.
So, it's really, really tough, but hopefully

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:33.430
with the breakthrough we've got on pensions,
it's going to make a big difference this winter.

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:37.210
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Excellent. Thanks
very much, Amanda.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:40.240
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Okay.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Julian, finance.

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:45.000
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: On the financial position
we've circulated a paper. Year–to–date

00:43:45.000 --> 00:43:52.680
the financial plan is holding. I mean, we
have an adverse variant when you look at provider

00:43:52.680 --> 00:43:58.890
and commissioner spend of £130 million at
the half–way point. You know, in context,

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:06.610
of course, we've spent £60 billion. So, that's
like a 0.2% variance. The official forecast

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:12.869
that we are getting back from providers and
commissioners is to broadly hold to plan.

00:44:12.869 --> 00:44:18.210
There is clearly a significant amount of risk
and we are working with a number of individual

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:24.030
organisations and systems where we've got
material risk to make sure that we are bringing

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:32.210
them in on–plan or as close to plan as we
can. Not just so that we balance this year's

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:39.620
budget, but critically as well, so that we
head into next year and the sort of, medium-term

00:44:39.620 --> 00:44:46.210
position in a good state of health. And actually,
so that we can continue to make the – you

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:51.310
know, maintain and in increase the investment
in some of the things we have already spoken

00:44:51.310 --> 00:44:57.200
about.
Worth saying on capital that we are seeing

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:07.650
the typical profile of capital spend relative
to budget but relative to last year we have

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:17.160
spent about £300 million more than at the
same point last year. Crucially with the settlement

00:45:17.160 --> 00:45:23.510
we have from the government, this means Trusts
with their own cash resources can deliver

00:45:23.510 --> 00:45:32.560
as fast as they possibly can the plans that
they have. We are managing to release more

00:45:32.560 --> 00:45:37.410
capital to Trusts who don't have their own
cash resources to deal with some of the critical

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:45.520
backlog issues, and there is no break on that
process as well as progressing the transformation

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:51.130
projects already agreed, and for example the
increased investment that the government announced

00:45:51.130 --> 00:45:58.910
in diagnostics, where we have now agreed the
organisations and I think now notified them.

00:45:58.910 --> 00:46:05.290
I'll check on that – the money that they
will get this year to buy new scanners to

00:46:05.290 --> 00:46:11.600
replace those which they are using which are
already passed their, sort of, service dates.

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:17.080
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Julian. Richard, you've
been around the block a few times on NHS finances.

00:46:17.080 --> 00:46:21.220
We're now halfway through the year, how do
you think – how do you feel about it?

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:27.360
&gt;&gt;Richard Douglas: I feel – I'm always careful
when I say this. I feel reasonably comfortable

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:31.410
with these figures. I mean, I think always
on these you look at them and you've got very

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:35.849
– what looks like very big numbers but there
incredibly small percentages. You're playing

00:46:35.849 --> 00:46:41.800
with your tiny little merchants, so when Julian
says to you, we've got a variance of about

00:46:41.800 --> 00:46:47.000
70 odd million, I mean this is absolutely
tiny. So, at the moment, this looks like it's

00:46:47.000 --> 00:46:52.330
broadly running to plan. And on the capital
side it's interesting because the – I was

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:55.960
a little bit sceptical at the start of the
year about our ability to spend the capital

00:46:55.960 --> 00:47:01.510
in the place that we wanted, but it looks
now like were about 25% up on the spend this

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:07.040
time last year Julian? So, yes, the ability
to actually utilise the capital was one of

00:47:07.040 --> 00:47:10.940
my concerns at the start of the year that
we're granted and then we don't spend it.

00:47:10.940 --> 00:47:16.020
Now, what this is showing is that I think
we are finding good uses for that. So, that

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:22.280
I find actually more reassuring even than
the revenue position.

00:47:22.280 --> 00:47:27.580
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Yes, thanks. Any other questions
for Julian? No. Excellent. Dido, have you

00:47:27.580 --> 00:47:31.170
got anything you wanted to, sort of, finish
up with?

00:47:31.170 --> 00:47:37.140
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Well, maybe just two observations.
The first one is just very mindful that because

00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:41.440
of the general election that this is quite
a, sort of, short and limited agenda, and

00:47:41.440 --> 00:47:47.950
I wouldn't want anybody to think that it reflects
the lack of work from the boards or the executive

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:52.660
teams or the teams within NHS England and
NHS Improvement. We know - a number of us

00:47:52.660 --> 00:47:58.599
have mentioned how hard we see colleagues
across the NHS working. I think it's worth

00:47:58.599 --> 00:48:03.500
also recognising how hard colleagues in NHS
England and NHS Improvement are working, both

00:48:03.500 --> 00:48:11.500
on the short-term support to systems and organisations
working on the long-term plan, implementation

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:16.280
planning, but also going through their own
journey as we bring our organisations together.

00:48:16.280 --> 00:48:20.760
So, we haven't mentioned that in this meeting,
but I think we should acknowledge that and

00:48:20.760 --> 00:48:25.860
acknowledge the work that is going on across
the service on the people plan, as I said

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:31.960
on bringing together those local system plans
to build up long-term implementation plans

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:38.510
that I'm sure will be in the public domain
in the New Year. But I just think we should

00:48:38.510 --> 00:48:44.560
acknowledge all that work that is happening
while we are necessarily brief in a public

00:48:44.560 --> 00:48:48.230
board meeting during an election.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Well, thank you all.

00:48:48.230 --> 00:48:52.290
Any other business anyone wanted to raise?
Anything? A different item? No. Good, well

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:53.580
thank you very much. Thank you.

